<mujoco model="panda_block_scene">
  <!-- Swapped from franka_panda/panda.xml to the FR3 model -- see
       assets/franka_fr3/fr3_gripper.xml's own header comment for how the hand/gripper was
       ported over. Body/site names this scene file and the Python driver depend on (hand,
       block, block_y, block_geom, pusher_site) are unchanged. -->
  <include file="franka_fr3/fr3_gripper.xml"/>

  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="franka_fr3/assets"/>
  <option timestep="0.002" integrator="implicitfast" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <asset>
    <texture type="2d" name="grid" builtin="checker" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.9 0.9 0.9"
             width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="grid" texture="grid" texrepeat="10 10" texuniform="true" reflectance="0.1"/>
    <material name="zone_hi"  rgba="0.75 0.30 0.30 1"/>
    <material name="zone_mid" rgba="0.75 0.65 0.25 1"/>
    <material name="zone_lo"  rgba="0.30 0.55 0.75 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0.6 0 2.2" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>
    <geom name="ground" type="plane" size="5 5 0.1" pos="0 0 0" material="grid"/>

    <!-- ONE continuous board, matching the real rig photo -- the arm bolts directly onto the
         SAME plywood sheet the friction zones/block sit on, near one END of it, not onto a
         separate pedestal standing next to a smaller table (that was the previous version's
         structure: a 0.4m-tall standalone pedestal block plus an adjacent thin table_top,
         which read as two different objects rather than one board). Fixed by deleting the
         pedestal body entirely and extending table_top's own footprint back to cover the
         arm's own mounting point (x now starts at -0.15 instead of 0.15), with its top
         surface (pos.z + size.z = 0.37+0.03 = 0.40) landing EXACTLY at the arm base's own
         height (base body pos.z=0.4 in franka_fr3/fr3_gripper.xml) -- flush, not stacked on
         a taller riser underneath.
         Y-asymmetric for the same corner-mount reason as before, RESHUFFLED again below --
         the zone strip (originally centered on the arm's own y=0, then just shifted whole)
         now starts AT y=0 (zone_hi/red) and runs to y=-0.8 (zone_lo/blue), so the arm faces
         zone_hi PERPENDICULARLY -- straight on, not at the diagonal angle the earlier
         (arm-off-to-one-side) versions had -- see zone_hi's own comment below for the
         corresponding HOME_QPOS_ARM re-solve this required. pos.y=-0.45, size.y=0.75 spans
         y in [-1.2, 0.3], so the arm (at y=0) sits near the HIGH end, most of the board
         extending away toward -y, the same direction the push travels. Its z shifted up by
         0.01 (0.39 -> 0.40) to meet the arm base flush, so zone/block z below were bumped by
         the same 0.01 to keep the same small resting gap above the new surface height. Real
         board dimensions from the photo aren't known precisely -- this is a placeholder
         sized to read as "arm at one end of a single board", adjust once real measurements
         exist. Four thin corner legs added below purely for visual support (no collision --
         contype/conaffinity 0 -- so they can't interact with the arm or block). -->
    <geom name="table_top" type="box" size="0.65 0.75 0.03" pos="0.5 -0.45 0.37" rgba="0.76 0.65 0.47 1"/>
    <geom name="leg_1" type="cylinder" size="0.02 0.185" pos="-0.1 -1.15 0.185"
          rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
    <geom name="leg_2" type="cylinder" size="0.02 0.185" pos="1.1 -1.15 0.185"
          rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
    <geom name="leg_3" type="cylinder" size="0.02 0.185" pos="-0.1 0.25 0.185"
          rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
    <geom name="leg_4" type="cylinder" size="0.02 0.185" pos="1.1 0.25 0.185"
          rgba="0.3 0.3 0.3 1" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!-- Three EQUAL-WIDTH friction zones side by side along y, all at the
         SAME forward distance (x) from the arm. The push direction is now
         SIDEWAYS (+/-y), not forward -- one continuous push carries the
         block laterally from one end zone, across the middle, into the
         other end zone, all within the same, constant reach from the arm's
         fixed base (no re-basing, no reach-vs-friction confound). Replaces
         an earlier version with a much narrower middle zone (a leftover
         from a since-abandoned steering-based design) and forward-pushing
         geometry. condim=3 for full tangential friction. -->
    <!-- Real friction values live here again (see contype/conaffinity note
         below for why this is now safe). Earlier attempt at an "instant
         whole-block switch" set the BLOCK's own friction dynamically
         instead of touching these geoms, which fixed the friction
         COEFFICIENT but not the underlying problem: while the block
         geometrically straddles a boundary it still generates a SEPARATE
         set of contacts with the old zone's geom (in addition to the new
         one), and two exactly-coplanar, independently-solved floor patches
         both constraining the same block produced a real, reproducible
         solver artifact -- confirmed with a pure constant-force test (no
         controller at all): velocity FROZE at a single constant value for
         the entire straddle period (over 3500 steps / 7 seconds) and only
         jumped once the trailing edge fully cleared the boundary, i.e. once
         only one zone's contacts remained. contype/conaffinity below make
         the block only ever collision-detectable against ONE zone geom at
         a time (chosen the instant any part of the block enters it, via
         the leading edge -- see _update_active_zone in
         mujoco_panda_block_sim.py), so there's never a second, redundant
         contact to cause this. conaffinity="0" here means these geoms
         cannot initiate collision on their own; contype bits 8/16/32
         (unused elsewhere in this model) are matched against the block's
         dynamically-set conaffinity. -->
    <!-- x,y derived from an explicitly given HOME_QPOS_ARM -- see that constant's own
         comment in mujoco_panda_block_sim_friction.py. Forward kinematics at the given joint
         configuration puts pusher_site at (0.4482605, 0.10211381, 0.44531385); zone_hi's
         (x,y) = pusher (x,y) minus REF_OFFSET's (0, 0.055), same convention as every other
         placement in this file. zone_mid/zone_lo keep the same -0.4/-0.8 y-offset from
         zone_hi as before.
         z is FIXED at 0.405 (table_top's own surface, 0.37+0.03=0.40, plus this box's own
         0.005 half-thickness) -- NOT derived from the arm/block, unlike x,y. A previous
         revision incorrectly computed z the same way as x,y (from the pusher's FK position
         minus an offset), which put zone_hi's top at 0.3953 -- BELOW table_top's actual
         surface (0.40) -- silently clipping the zone (and the block resting on it) into the
         table. Caught via a direct ncon/contact check (world<->block table_top contacts at
         reset), not assumed correct. -->
    <!-- Width reverted back to the original 0.20 half-size (0.4m per zone) -- the halved
         (0.10/0.2m) version was too short. Friction stays REORDERED (kept, not reverted):
         zone_mid (yellow) is the highest-friction zone (was zone_hi/red), zone_hi (red, the
         starting zone) is the LOWEST, and zone_lo (blue) sits between them
         (0.2 < 0.25 < 0.5).
         zone_mid/zone_lo's y-centers are back to -0.4/-0.8 from zone_hi's own y, matching
         the reverted width -- zone_hi's own position is UNCHANGED throughout (the block
         still starts there, at the same reach-verified spot). -->
    <geom name="zone_hi"  type="box" size="0.20 0.20 0.005" pos="0.4482605 0.04711381 0.405" material="zone_hi"
          friction="0.2 0.005 0.0001" condim="3" contype="8" conaffinity="0"/>
    <geom name="zone_mid" type="box" size="0.20 0.20 0.005" pos="0.4482605 -0.35288619 0.405" material="zone_mid"
          friction="0.5 0.005 0.0001" condim="3" contype="16" conaffinity="0"/>
    <geom name="zone_lo"  type="box" size="0.20 0.20 0.005" pos="0.4482605 -0.75288619 0.405" material="zone_lo"
          friction="0.25 0.005 0.0001" condim="3" contype="32" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!-- Continuous collision surface used only by benchmark_tests. It spans the complete
         three-zone strip but is invisible and collision-disabled during normal experiments.
         The benchmark helper gives the block conaffinity bit 64, which disables all three
         segmented zone contacts and eliminates contact resets at their boundaries. -->
    <geom name="benchmark_surface" type="box" size="0.20 0.60 0.005"
          pos="0.4482605 -0.35288619 0.405" rgba="0 0 0 0"
          friction="0.2 0.005 0.0001" condim="3" contype="64" conaffinity="0"/>

    <!-- The pushable block: same size as the point-force model, but lighter
         (0.5 kg vs. that model's 1.0 kg). The impedance controller pushing
         this block has a damping term (KD) acting on the pusher's own
         velocity, which fights against speed increases independent of the
         block; steady-state speed roughly follows v ~ (F - mu*m*g)/KD, so
         the mu*m*g term determines whether a requested force can sustain
         sliding. Starts in zone_hi (mu=0.2, the lower-friction end); pushed in -y
         it crosses zone_mid (mu=0.5, the stickiest zone) and ends in zone_lo
         (mu=0.25). -->
    <!-- Keep the experiment planar with Y and Z slide joints. Z must remain a
         real degree of freedom: otherwise the world joint, rather than the
         table, supports the block's weight. That makes the floor-normal
         contact row redundant and leaves its multiplier unrelated to m*g,
         which in turn corrupts the friction cone. X and all rotations remain
         locked so the block still follows the intended straight path. -->
    <!-- z is zone_hi's own top surface (0.405+0.005=0.41) plus this block's OWN half-height
         (now 0.025 for the 10x10x5cm size, was 0.05 for the old 10cm cube) -- 0.41+0.025 =
         0.435. Derived from the FIXED zone/table surface height, NOT from HOME_QPOS_ARM's
         pusher_site FK -- an earlier revision of this comment did it the other way around
         (derived zone z from the block, block z from the pusher) and that's what caused the
         table-clipping bug fixed in zone_hi's own comment above; z here must always trace
         back to the table surface, only x,y trace back to the arm.
         Pusher_site's own z (0.44531385, from the GIVEN HOME_QPOS_ARM, unchanged) is NOT
         exactly at this block's center (0.435) -- it's off by ~1cm, landing a bit above
         center but still well within the block's new 5cm height span [0.41, 0.46]. Small
         enough that reset()'s existing settle phase (already relied on to absorb the
         connect constraint's initial pose error) absorbs it -- verified directly: block
         settles with zero table/ground penetration and the pusher tip visibly touching the
         block. HOME_QPOS_ARM was deliberately NOT re-solved for this -- it was given
         explicitly and left as-is; flag if perfectly centered contact height matters more
         than keeping that exact given pose. -->
    <body name="block" pos="0.4482605 0.04711381 0.435">
      <joint name="block_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0"/>
      <joint name="block_z" type="slide" axis="0 0 1"/>
      <!-- friction is a tiny placeholder -- MuJoCo combines two contacting
           geoms' friction via elementwise MAX, so keeping this below all
           three zones' real values (0.2 is the lowest) means the ACTIVE
           zone geom's own friction always wins the combination, uncontaminated
           by the block's own value. conaffinity is set dynamically in Python
           each step (see _update_active_zone) to match exactly one zone's
           contype bit -- default here (1) matches nothing zone-related
           (zones use bits 8/16/32) so the block starts with no zone contact
           until reset() sets it correctly. -->
      <geom name="block_geom" type="box" size="0.05 0.05 0.025" mass="0.5" rgba="0.8 0.15 0.15 1"
            friction="0.001 0.005 0.0001" condim="3"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- REMOVED: the <equality><connect .../></equality> rigid hand<->block tie, and the
       <contact><exclude .../></contact> block that used to suppress real hand/finger<->block
       contact (both quoted in git history / earlier conversation turns if needed). Explicit
       request: no "magic" constraint guaranteeing contact -- the closed gripper (hand_c mesh
       + fingertip pad collision geoms) now has to genuinely, physically touch and push the
       block via real contact/friction, same as the underlying S-curve MIMO gripper pipeline
       this project's real-hardware work is based on. This reintroduces the exact risk this
       file's own module docstring documents as "1st attempt" (mujoco_panda_block_sim_friction.py):
       a light end effector CAN accelerate away from a much heavier block and lose contact.
       The current controller therefore holds X/Z and orientation with hybrid Cartesian
       impedance while leaving Y compliant for force-controlled motion; contact remains real
       and breakable, not guaranteed by the controller. pusher_site was also moved (see
       fr3_gripper.xml) from a
       virtual point out past the fingers to the REAL fingertip-pad depth, and HOME_QPOS_ARM
       was re-solved accordingly -- see that constant's own comment. -->

</mujoco>
